@test
fn test_the_one_value_that_is_not_cpythons_answer() [io]
    // `1.7e-308` is SUBNORMAL, and a subnormal result is rounded twice
    // on the way out of this module: once when the hundred-bit pair is
    // brought back from the range it was computed in, and once into
    // the reduced precision a subnormal has.  Two roundings can land
    // one step off one, and here they do — so the two-digit form
    // CPython writes is not one this module's own parser reads back as
    // the value, and the search goes on to seventeen digits.
    //
    // What it writes is CORRECT: it reads back as the same double,
    // through this package and through CPython.  It is not SHORTEST,
    // and it is the only value in a 497-value corpus for which that is
    // true.  Pinned here by name so that a second one is a failure
    // rather than a quiet companion.
    test.case("1.7e-308")
    test.assert_eq(numstr.float_to_str(1.7e-308), "1.7000000000000002e-308")
    test.assert(real.parse_float("1.7000000000000002e-308").num == 1.7e-308)
